Superluminous supernovae (SLSNe) are stellar explosions whose peak optical luminosities exceed those of ordinary core-collapse and thermonuclear supernovae by roughly an order of magnitude or more. They are observationally divided into a hydrogen-poor (Type I/Ic) subclass and a hydrogen-rich subclass. Three principal physical mechanisms have been proposed to account for their extreme luminosities:

(i) radioactive decay of several solar masses of ⁵⁶Ni synthesized in a pair-instability explosion of a very massive star;
(ii) efficient conversion of ejecta kinetic energy into radiation through interaction of the ejecta with dense circumstellar matter;
(iii) sustained energy injection by the spin-down of a newly born, rapidly rotating (millisecond-period) highly magnetized neutron star (magnetar).

Determine, for each of the two SLSN subclasses (hydrogen-poor Type Ic and hydrogen-rich), which of the above mechanisms (or which combination thereof) is the actual dominant power source. In particular, for the hydrogen-poor Type Ic SLSNe under the magnetar hypothesis, decide whether their progenitors are chemically homogeneously evolving, rapidly rotating, low-metallicity massive stars, and provide observational identification of such progenitors. For the hydrogen-rich SLSNe, identify the progenitor systems and the mechanism responsible for their high luminosities.

